{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-95b5a48af2a30449", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t\n2\t\n3\tAnomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\n4\t\n5\t Title:\n6\t\n7\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n8\t\n9\t Description:\n10\t\n11\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n12\t Learn more\n13\t ×\n14\t\n15\t License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n16\t\n17\tarXiv:2608.00447v1 [eess.AS] 01 Aug 2026\n18\t\n19\t\\NoHyper\n20\t\n21\tAnomalous Sound Detection Meets Noise-Aware Self-Supervised Learning\n22\t\n23\tAbstract\n24\tIn this paper, we introduce noise-aware self-supervised learning (NA-SSL) models for noise-aware anomalous sound detection (NA-ASD).\n25\tNA-ASD is an ASD task with two-channel audio recordings, where one microphone is located close to the target machine and the other is located farther away to capture noise.\n26\tFor this task, we simulate two-channel recordings using diverse audio datasets and train NA-SSL models to extract clean SSL representations of the close-microphone signal by using the far-microphone recording dominated by background noise as auxiliary information.\n27\tThe NA-SSL models are then used as frontends in the standard ASD framework.\n28\tOur experimental evaluation on the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 development dataset demonstrates the effectiveness of the NA-SSL framework across three base SSL models (BEATs, EAT, and Dasheng), both with and without discriminative fine-tuning.\n29\tFurthermore, the challenge results proved the effectiveness of the proposed approach, where the NA-BEATs system won the challenge by a large margin, achieving an official score of 70.24%, while the second-place system achieved 65.46%.\n30\t\n31\t1 Introduction\n32\t\n33\t\\Ac\n34\tasd aims to detect mechanical failures from machine sounds [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8].\n35\tSince it is infeasible to exhaustively collect rare and diverse anomalous sounds, ASD systems are developed using only normal sounds.\n36\tOne major challenge in ASD is the presence of severe background noise in factory environments during both training data collection and testing.\n37\tMotivated by this challenge, noise-aware (NA-ASD) considers the use of auxiliary noise information [7].\n38\t\\Acnaasd employs a practical two-microphone recording setup, where one microphone is placed close to the target machine and the other is placed farther away.\n39\tAlthough the far-microphone signal can also contain the target machine sound, the target sound is less dominant than in the close-microphone signal, making the far-microphone signal a useful auxiliary source of noise information.\n40\tThe NA-ASD task was newly introduced in DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 [7], and there is strong interest in developing ASD systems that can effectively utilize such auxiliary noise information.\n41\t\n42\tA recent trend in standard ASD tasks is the use of audio SSL models [9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 8]. \\Acssl models effectively capture subtle differences between normal and anomalous sounds, enabling training-free ASD by simply computing distances between a test sample and normal training samples in the representation space [9, 10, 11].\n43\t\\Acssl models fine-tuned on machine sound datasets through classification tasks using machine-information labels capture more machine-specific information and achieve state-of-the-art performance [12, 13, 8].\n44\tHowever, under noisy conditions, such representations essentially encode irrelevant background noise as well, making it difficult to focus on target machine sounds.\n45\tOne possible solution is denoising training specialized for extracting specific target machine sounds, as in WavLM [14].\n46\tHowever, it is not applicable to ASD tasks because clean target machine sounds are unavailable as training data.\n47\t\n48\tA promising approach to handling noisy conditions in audio SSL models is the noise-aware (NA-SSL) framework [15].\n49\t\\Acnassl models are pre-trained on diverse audio signals to extract clean representations from noisy signals using auxiliary noise inputs.\n50\tBecause NA-SSL models perform conditional denoising based on the provided auxiliary noise information, they do not necessarily require clean target sounds or noise samples specific to the downstream task as pre-training data.\n51\tIn [15], one specific implementation of the NA-SSL framework was investigated, in which NA-BEATs was built on BEATs [16] and noise-only segments extracted from noisy input signals were used as auxiliary noise inputs.\n52\tMore generally, the framework is expected to accommodate any auxiliary signal that captures noise information, such as the far-microphone signal available in the NA-ASD task.\n53\t\n54\tProjectionEncoderNA Layer N\\times NA-SSLSSLOriginalSSLlayersAdditionaltrainablelayerSharedweightsNoisy far-micrecordingNoisy close-micrecordingCleantargetMSE \\mathbf{r} \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} \n55\tFigure 1: \n56\tOverview of NA-SSL, adapted from [15]. The model estimates clean SSL representations \\mathbf{r} from two-channel noisy signals, with one microphone placed close to the target sound source and the other placed farther away to capture background noise information.\n57\t\n58\tIn this paper, we explore the use of NA-SSL models for the NA-ASD task.\n59\tTo this end, we simulate two-channel recordings using diverse audio signals and pre-train NA-SSL models to extract clean representations from two-channel noisy signals, as shown in Fig. 1.\n60\tThe NA-SSL models learn conditional denoising by exploiting the difference between the two channels: target sound information is more prominent in the close-microphone signal than in the far-microphone signal, whereas noise information is more prominent in the far-microphone signal.\n61\tThrough this conditional denoising scheme, NA-SSL models can provide denoised representations for the downstream ASD task without requiring clean target machine sounds as pre-training data.\n62\tFurthermore, in addition to NA-BEATs, we newly design NA extensions of EAT [17] and Dasheng [18].\n63\tExperimental evaluations on the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 development dataset show that NA-SSL substantially improves performance across the three base SSL models, both with and without discriminative fine-tuning.\n64\tIn the official challenge evaluation, NA-BEATs with discriminative fine-tuning achieved the best performance among 175 system submissions, with an official score of 70.24%, whereas the second-place system achieved 65.46% and the official baseline system achieved 59.80%.\n65\t\n66\t2 SSL-based ASD\n67\t\n68\tTypical ASD systems consist of a frontend and a backend [8].\n69\tThe frontend extracts representations from machine sounds, and the backend computes anomaly scores based on distances in the representation space.\n70\tRecent studies have extensively investigated audio SSL models as frontends [9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 8], along with advances in backend techniques [9, 10, 11].\n71\t\n72\t2.1 SSL models\n73\t\n74\tBEATs [16] is an audio SSL model widely adopted for ASD [9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 8]. It performs masked-prediction-based SSL in a discrete token space.\n75\tA key feature of BEATs is its iterative training pipeline, in which the acoustic tokenizer and the SSL model are alternately refined through knowledge distillation.\n76\tEAT [17] has also demonstrated strong ASD performance [10, 11, 8]. It is based on a masked bootstrapping framework, in which a student model is trained to reconstruct the latent representations of a teacher model at masked positions.\n77\tIt also introduces utterance-level losses and a CLS token to capture global information.\n78\tDasheng [18] is a recent SSL model based on a masked autoencoder framework and is trained on 272k hours of diverse audio data.\n79\tDasheng takes mel-spectrogram segments as input, whereas BEATs and EAT take mel-spectrogram patches as input.\n80\tThese models convert the input sequence into a representation sequence \\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D} , where L denotes the number of mel-spectrogram patches or segments and D denotes the representation dimension.\n81\tThe representation is then either directly used in the backend or further fine-tuned on machine sound datasets.\n82\t\n83\t2.2 Discriminative fine-tuning\n84\t\n85\tFine-tuning is performed based on a classification task using machine-information labels [12, 13].\n86\tIn recent DCASE datasets [5, 6, 7], the machine-information labels in the training data include the machine type (e.g., bearing), domain (source or target), and machine-dependent fine-grained attribute labels (e.g., rotation speed).\n87\tHere, different attributes are included in the source and target domains.\n88\tAlso, some machine types do not have attribute labels.\n89\tFor such machine types, pseudo-labeling is employed [19, 13].\n90\tPseudo attribute labels are generated by applying clustering to the representations of the training data.\n91\tThe pseudo attribute labels are then combined with the other available coarse labels of the target machine (i.e., the machine-type and domain labels) and the attribute labels of the other machines to form a multi-class classification task.\n92\t\n93\t2.3 Backend techniques\n94\t\n95\tIn the backend, the SSL representation sequence \\mathbf{r} is aggregated to calculate the anomaly score.\n96\tSaengthong et al. [9] proposed an aggregation technique that utilizes frequency information.\n97\tThis technique reshapes \\mathbf{r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D} into \\mathbf{r}_{\\rm TF}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T\\times F\\times D} , where T and F denote the numbers of mel-spectrogram patches along the time and frequency axes, respectively.\n98\tIt then applies average pooling along the time axis and concatenates the frequency axis to obtain a single FD -dimensional frequency-preserving representation.\n99\tThis representation was shown to improve backend performance compared with a global representation averaged over both the time and frequency axes [9].\n100\t\n101\tBEAM [10] is another technique that builds on a frequency-preserving representation.\n102\tIt constructs a D -dimensional representation memory bank for each frequency index.\n103\tAn anomaly score is calculated separately for each frequency index and then averaged across the frequency indices to obtain a sample-level anomaly score.\n104\tThis technique mitigates undesirably high anomaly scores caused by variations within normal sounds.\n105\tFor example, an unseen combination of machine sound and noise can yield a high anomaly score, even when each frequency component has been separately observed in the training data.\n106\tBy avoiding strict joint comparison across frequencies, this technique can improve robustness to normal variations while retaining sensitivity to anomalies in each frequency.\n107\t\n108\t\\Ac\n109\trdp [11] is a pooling technique designed as an alternative to simple average pooling.\n110\tIt computes a weighted average of the representation sequence, assigning each representation \\mathbf{r}_{l} a weight w_{l}={(1+\\hat{d}_{l})^{\\gamma}}/{\\sum_{l^{\\prime}}(1+\\hat{d}_{l^{\\prime}})^{\\gamma}} where \\hat{d}_{l} is the Euclidean distance between \\mathbf{r}_{l} and the sequence average, normalized by the maximum such distance within the sequence, and \\gamma is a hyperparameter.\n111\tThis emphasizes representations that deviate from the average, thereby preserving temporally localized anomalies that are smoothed out by typical average pooling.\n112\t\n113\t3 Proposed method\n114\t\n115\tWe introduce NA-SSL models to the NA-ASD task.\n116\tWe follow the training procedure of the previous NA-BEATs implementation [15] but modify the auxiliary noise condition to match the NA-ASD task.\n117\tAlso, we design multiple NA-SSL models.\n118\tSpecifically, we design NA-BEATs, NA-EAT, and NA-Dasheng in the same manner based on BEATs (BEATs_iter3.pt), EAT (EAT-base_epoch30_pretrain), and Dasheng (dasheng-base), respectively.\n119\t\n120\t3.1 Noise-aware self-supervised learning (NA-SSL)\n121\t\n122\tFollowing [15], we train an NA-SSL model by distillation with a mean squared error (MSE) loss between the clean target representation sequence \\mathbf{r}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D} from the frozen original SSL model and the estimated representation sequence \\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D}  [15], given by\n123\t\n124\t \\displaystyle\\mathbf{r}\\! \n125\t \\displaystyle=\\!\\mathrm{SSL}(\\mathbf{s}), \n126\t\n127\t(1)\n128\t\n129\t \\displaystyle\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}\\! \n130\t \\displaystyle=\\!\\mathrm{NASSL}({\\mathbf{x}},\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}), \n131\t\n132\t(2)\n133\t\n134\twhere \\mathbf{s} , \\mathbf{x} , and \\mathbf{n}^{\\prime} denote clean target sound, noisy target sound, and auxiliary noise signal, respectively.\n135\tFor the NA-ASD task, we use the close-microphone signal as \\mathbf{x} and the far-microphone signal as \\mathbf{n}^{\\prime} .\n136\tWe define the clean target sound \\mathbf{s} as the signal obtained at the close microphone using a room impulse response including early reflections within 50 ms.\n137\tFor NA-EAT, which produces a CLS token in addition to the representation sequence, we apply the MSE loss to both outputs with equal weights.\n138\t\n139\tFor all base SSL models, we construct NA extensions by inserting trainable NA layers into the frozen original SSL model after each Transformer layer, as in [15].\n140\tHere, BEATs, EAT, and Dasheng all have a projection layer and 12 Transformer layers.\n141\tBoth the close-microphone signal \\mathbf{x} and the far-microphone signal \\mathbf{n}^{\\prime} are passed through the original base SSL model.\n142\tAfter each original Transformer layer, an NA layer refines the noisy target representation \\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D} using the corresponding noise representation \\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D} .\n143\tSpecifically, the cross-attention-based NA layer works as follows [15]:\n144\t\n145\t \\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}} \n146\t \\displaystyle\\xleftarrow{}\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathrm{MHCA}(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}),\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{n}^{\\prime}})), \n147\t\n148\t(3)\n149\t\n150\t \\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}} \n151\t \\displaystyle\\xleftarrow{}\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathrm{FFN}_{\\mathrm{SwiGLU}}(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(\\mathbf{z}_{\\mathbf{x}})), \n152\t\n153\t(4)\n154\t\n155\twhere \\mathrm{MHCA} denotes multi-head cross-attention [20], with the first argument used as the query and the second argument used as the key and value.\n156\t \\mathrm{RMSNorm} denotes root mean square normalization [21], and \\mathrm{FFN}_{\\mathrm{SwiGLU}} denotes a Swish gated linear unit (SwiGLU)-based feed-forward network (FFN) [22].\n157\tThe NA layer incorporates auxiliary noise information to refine the noisy representation and was shown to improve performance under noisy conditions in [15].\n158\t\n159\t3.2 Simulation of two-channel recordings\n160\t\n161\tFor training the NA-SSL models, we simulate two-channel recordings using diverse audio signals.\n162\tFSD50K [23] is used as the target-sound dataset, while WHAM!48kHz [24], DEMAND [25], and QUT-NOISE [26] are used as the noise datasets.\n163\tAll signals are resampled to 16 kHz and randomly cropped or zero-padded to 10 s.\n164\t\n165\tWe simulate room impulse responses using Pyroomacoustics [27], as shown in Fig. 2.\n166\tThe reverberation time is randomly sampled from [0.1, 0.4] s.\n167\tThe target sound source is randomly placed with a 0.5 m margin from the walls.\n168\tThe close microphone is fixed 0.05 m from the target source, while the far microphone is randomly placed [0.1, 1.0] m away.\n169\tFour different noise signals are randomly selected and played, each placed 0.2 m away from a corner of the room.\n170\tThe SNR at the close microphone is randomly selected from [-10, 10] dB.\n171\t\n172\t3.3 ASD procedure using NA-SSL models\n173\t\n174\t\\Ac\n175\tnassl models can be combined with well-established SSL-based ASD techniques described in Section 2.\n176\tFor the ASD backend, we simply use the estimated clean representation \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} instead of the noisy representation.\n177\tSince the output of an NA-SSL model has the same format as that of the original SSL model, any backend technique designed for SSL representations can be directly applied to NA-SSL representations.\n178\t\n179\tWe can also perform discriminative fine-tuning of NA-SSL on top of \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} , where both the SSL model and the NA layers are updated.\n180\tWhile the base SSL model is shared between the close- and far-microphone signals, the NA layers refine only the close-microphone representation.\n181\tTherefore, fine-tuning the NA layers allows them to adapt specifically to the close-microphone representation, which predominantly contains the target machine sound.\n182\t\n183\t \\sim\\mathcal{U}(3.0,8.0)  m \\sim\\mathcal{U}(3.0,8.0)  m0.5 m0.2 m0.05 m \\sim\\mathcal{U}(0.1,1.0)  mTCFT: target sourceC: close micF: far mic: noise sources\n184\tFigure 2: \n185\tRoom geometry for simulation.\n186\tThe room height is sampled from \\mathcal{U}(1.5,3.0)  m, and all sources and microphones are placed at a height of 1.0 m.\n187\t\n188\tTable 1: \n189\tEvaluation of frontends across three base SSL models.\n190\tThe values are official scores, and Total shows the harmonic mean of the scores over the machine types.\n191\tThe mean, CI95, and ensemble results across the three trials are reported.\n192\tThe backend consists of BEAM and RDP with \\gamma=4 .\n193\tNA denotes NA-SSL pre-training, and Dis denotes discriminative fine-tuning.\n194\t∗ denotes machine types without attribute labels.\n195\t\n196\tMean (CI95)\n197\t\n198\tEnsemble\n199\t\n200\tBase\n201\tMethod\n202\tbearingE