1\t\n372\tDasheng\n373\tOriginal\n374\t64.36 \n375\t\n376\t52.58 \n377\t\n378\t59.97 \n379\t\n380\t57.75 \n381\t\n382\t52.36 \n383\t\n384\t55.26 \n385\t\n386\t58.47 \n387\t\n388\t56.98 \n389\t\n390\t56.98\n391\t\n392\tNA\n393\t63.40 (2.14)\n394\t\n395\t53.92 (10.09)\n396\t\n397\t59.08 (8.00)\n398\t\n399\t65.65 (5.93)\n400\t\n401\t57.39 (1.74)\n402\t\n403\t54.58 (2.74)\n404\t\n405\t84.99 (13.63)\n406\t\n407\t61.33 (2.13)\n408\t\n409\t62.34\n410\t\n411\tDis\n412\t65.25 (2.61)\n413\t\n414\t52.95 (2.11)\n415\t\n416\t61.51 (1.02)\n417\t\n418\t57.49 (1.83)\n419\t\n420\t52.61 (0.55)\n421\t\n422\t54.35 (2.67)\n423\t\n424\t70.56 (3.39)\n425\t\n426\t58.60 (0.69)\n427\t\n428\t58.91\n429\t\n430\tDis NA\n431\t\n432\t66.81 (3.51)\n433\t\n434\t54.81 (4.87)\n435\t\n436\t60.82 (1.24)\n437\t\n438\t64.87 (0.25)\n439\t\n440\t52.93 (1.46)\n441\t\n442\t55.71 (3.03)\n443\t\n444\t90.03 (2.65)\n445\t\n446\t61.93 (2.13)\n447\t\n448\t63.10\n449\t\n450\t4 Experimental evaluation\n451\t\n452\tIn the experimental evaluation, we demonstrate the general effectiveness of the NA-SSL framework across diverse settings, including three base SSL models, multiple backend techniques, and configurations with and without discriminative fine-tuning.\n453\t\n454\t4.1 Setup\n455\t\n456\tWe conducted experimental evaluations using the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 dataset, compiled from ToyADMOS2 [28] and MIMII DG [29].\n457\tThe dataset consists of development and evaluation sets with distinct machine types.\n458\tThe development set includes seven machine types: bearingEmu, fan, gearboxEmu, valveEmu, sliderEmu, ToyCarEmu, and ToyCar.\n459\tThe evaluation set includes five machine types: ToyDrone, ToothBrush, SewingMachine, Sander, and BlowerDustCollector.\n460\tEach machine type has 1000 training samples and 200 test samples.\n461\tAll training samples are normal, with 99% from the source domain and 1% from the target domain.\n462\tAttribute labels are available only for the training data of the following machine types: fan, gearboxEmu, ToyCarEmu, ToyDrone, Sander, and BlowerDustCollector.\n463\tThe test data in the development set consist of 50 samples for each combination of domain and normal/anomalous class.\n464\tThe test data in the evaluation set are provided without domain and normal/anomalous labels for the official challenge evaluation.\n465\tEach recording is a 6–16-second two-channel signal sampled at 16 kHz.\n466\t\n467\tFor the NA layers, we set the number of heads in MHCA to 8 and the hidden size of the FFN to 2304.\n468\tWe trained the NA-SSL models for 200 epochs using the AdamW optimizer, a fixed learning rate of 0.0001, and a batch size of 160.\n469\tWe applied exponential moving average (EMA) with a decay rate of 0.999 after 20k training steps.\n470\t\n471\tFor discriminative fine-tuning, we generated pseudo labels for machine types without attribute labels.\n472\tWhen fine-tuning a given SSL or NA-SSL model, we generated the pseudo labels from that same model.\n473\tThe pseudo labels were generated by applying k-means clustering to the frequency-preserving representation.\n474\tFor each machine type, pseudo labels were generated in the source domain, where the number of clusters was set to 0.8% of the number of samples.\n475\tFor the target domain, all samples were assigned to a single target-domain class within each machine type.\n476\t\n477\tDuring the discriminative fine-tuning, we aggregated the representation sequence \\mathbf{r} into a single 256-dimensional discriminative feature using an attentive statistics pooling layer [30] and a linear layer.\n478\tIn the backend, the SSL representation sequence before aggregation was used rather than the resulting discriminative feature.\n479\tFor the fine-tuning of the original SSL models, we applied low-rank adaptation (LoRA) with a rank of 64 to the query, key, and value projection layers in the attention modules.\n480\tIn NA-SSL, LoRA was also applied to MHCA in the NA layers with the same configuration.\n481\tWe fine-tuned the models for 25 epochs using sub-cluster AdaCos [31] with 16 sub-clusters, mixup with a probability of 0.5, AdamW optimizer, and a batch size of 8.\n482\tThe learning rate was linearly increased from 0 to 0.0001 over the first 5,000 steps.\n483\t\n484\tWe evaluated the performance with multiple backend techniques: frequency-preserving representation, BEAM, and RDP.\n485\tTo address the data imbalance between the source and target domains, we always used variance-minimization-based score rescaling [32] with four nearest neighbors.\n486\tCosine distance was used to calculate the anomaly score.\n487\t\n488\tAs the evaluation metric, we used the official score [7] calculated as the harmonic mean of three types of AUC: (a) partial (pAUC) with p=0.1 , calculated using samples from both the source and target domains; and (b) source-domain and (c) target-domain AUCs, each calculated using normal samples from the corresponding domain and anomalous samples from both domains.\n489\tWe performed NA-SSL pre-training and discriminative fine-tuning for three trials with different random seeds.\n490\tWe report the mean and 95% confidence interval (CI95) of the official scores across the three trials and also report the ensemble result obtained by averaging the anomaly scores across the trials.\n491\t\n492\tTable 2: \n493\tEvaluation using multiple backends.\n494\tThe values are harmonic mean of the official scores over machine types in the development dataset.\n495\tThe values are reported as “Mean (CI95)”.\n496\tNA denotes NA-SSL pre-training, and Dis denotes discriminative fine-tuning.\n497\tGlobal and Freq denote global and frequency-preserving representations, respectively.\n498\tAP denotes the average pooling.\n499\tThe value in parentheses after “RDP” indicates \\gamma .\n500\tSince Dasheng extracts segment-wise representations, the frequency-preserving representation is identical to the global representation.\n501\t\n502\tBase\n503\t\n504\tMethod\n50