t\n715\t(2.13)\n716\t\n717\t61.75\n718\t\n719\t(0.64)\n720\t\n721\tDis\n722\t\n723\t58.33\n724\t\n725\t(0.42)\n726\t\n727\t-\n728\t\n729\t-\n730\t\n731\t58.60\n732\t\n733\t(0.69)\n734\t\n735\t59.07\n736\t\n737\t(0.92)\n738\t\n739\tDis NA\n740\t\n741\t61.72\n742\t\n743\t(2.53)\n744\t\n745\t-\n746\t\n747\t-\n748\t\n749\t61.93\n750\t\n751\t(2.13)\n752\t\n753\t62.63\n754\t\n755\t(1.89)\n756\t\n757\t4.2 Results\n758\t\n759\tTable 1 compares frontends using the same backend consisting of BEAM and RDP with \\gamma=4 .\n760\tFirst, we can see that NA-SSL is effective regardless of the base model and whether discriminative fine-tuning is applied.\n761\tNA-SSL yields substantial improvements of over 20% for valveEmu and also improves performance for the other machine types.\n762\tDiscriminative fine-tuning further improves performance and achieves the best performance for all base models.\n763\tAlso, by using pseudo labels, the benefits of discriminative fine-tuning can be observed even for machine types without attribute labels.\n764\tWe also observe high variance in ASD performance for NA-SSL models, highlighting the importance of ensembling multiple trials.\n765\t\n766\tTable 2 shows the results obtained using different backend techniques.\n767\tThe results demonstrate the effectiveness of the individual backend techniques and show that their benefits also carry over to NA-SSL-based frontends.\n768\tIn particular, we can see that BEAM substantially improves performance.\n769\tFor RDP, larger values of \\gamma yield greater improvements for BEATs and Dasheng, whereas RDP provides little benefit for EAT. These trends observed for RDP are consistent with the findings reported in [11].\n770\t\n771\tWe also conducted an ablation study to evaluate the effect of applying LoRA to the NA layers during discriminative fine-tuning, as shown in Table 3.\n772\tAlthough discriminative fine-tuning remains effective even without LoRA in the NA layers, applying LoRA to the NA layers tends to yield better performance.\n773\t\n774\tFigure 3 shows the results on the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 evaluation dataset reported in [33].\n775\tIt compares our Dis NA-BEATs with the ten top-performing systems [34, 35, 36, 37, 38, 39, 40, 41, 42] and the official baseline system [7]. Here, the backend consists of BEAM and RDP with \\gamma=4 , and the anomaly scores from three trials are ensembled.\n776\tDis NA-BEATs ranked first on the evaluation dataset with an official score of 70.24%, surpassing the second-place system (65.46%) and the official baseline system (59.80%) by a considerable margin.\n777\tIt is also noteworthy that our Dis NA-BEATs is built on only a single base SSL model with a single backend, whereas the other systems rely on ensembles of multiple SSL models [35, 40] and multiple backends [35, 38, 39, 40, 42].\n778\tMoreover, our Dis NA-BEATs outperforms the other BEATs-based systems [35, 39, 40, 42].\n779\t\n780\tTable 3: Ablation study of discriminative fine-tuning.\n781\tThe values are harmonic mean of the official scores over machine types in the development dataset.\n782\tThe values are reported as “Mean (CI95)”.\n783\tThe backend consists of BEAM and RDP with \\gamma=4 .\n784\tNA denotes NA-SSL pre-training, and Dis denotes discriminative fine-tuning.\n785\t\n786\tMethod\n787\tBEATs\n788\tEAT\n789\tDasheng\n790\t\n791\tNA\n792\t63.64 (1.71)\n793\t\n794\t62.74 (1.41)\n795\t\n796\t61.33 (2.13)\n797\t\n798\tDis NA\n799\t\n800\t64.32 (0.77)\n801\t\n802\t63.88 (0.30)\n803\t\n804\t61.93 (2.13)\n805\t\n806\tDis NA w/o LoRA in NA layers\n807\t63.97 (1.81)\n808\t\n809\t63.17 (1.17)\n810\t\n811\t62.09 (0.84)\n812\t\n813\t Dis NABEATs 70.24 % 70.24 %[34] 65.46 % 65.46 %[35] 65.45 % 65.45 %[36] 64.43 % 64.43 %[37] 64.30 % 64.30 %[38] 63.71 % 63.71 %[39] 62.99 % 62.99 %[40] 62.59 % 62.59 %[41] 62.40 % 62.40 %[42] 62.36 % 62.36 %Base-line 59.80 % 59.80 %606570Official score\n814\t\n815\tFigure 3: \n816\tComparison of our Dis NA-BEATs with the ten top-performing systems and the official baseline system on the DCASE 2026 Challenge Task 2 evaluation dataset.\n817\t\n818\t5 Conclusion\n819\t\n820\tIn this paper, we explored the use of NA-SSL models for the NA-ASD task.\n821\tThe NA-SSL models aim to extract clean SSL representations from noisy signals by leveraging auxiliary noise information captured by a microphone located farther from the target sound source.\n822\tThese models are first pre-trained on simulated two-channel recordings constructed from diverse audio signals.\n823\tThey are then used as frontends within a well-established SSL-based ASD framework.\n824\tOur experimental results demonstrated the general effectiveness and broad compatibility of the NA-SSL framework across three base SSL models, multiple backend methods, and configurations both with and without discriminative fine-tuning.\n825\t\n826\tREFERENCES\n827\t\n828\t[1]\n829\t\n830\tY. Koizumi, Y. Kawaguchi, K. Imoto, T. Nakamura, Y. Nikaido, R. Tanabe, H. Purohit, K. Suefusa, T. Endo, M. Yasuda et al., “Description and discussion on DCASE2020 challenge task2: Unsupervised anomalous sound detection for machine condition monitoring,” in Proc. DCASE, 2020, pp. 81–85.\n831\t\n832\t[2]\n833\t\n834\tY. Kawaguchi, K. Imoto, Y. Koizumi, N. Harada, D. Niizumi, K. Dohi, R. Tanabe, H. Purohit, and T. Endo, “Description and discussion on DCASE 2021 challenge task 2: Unsupervised anomalous detection for machine condition monitoring under domain shifted conditions,” in Proc. DCASE, 2021, pp. 186–190.\n835\t\n836\t[3]\n837\t\n838\tK. Dohi, K. Imoto, N. Harada, D. Niizumi, Y. Koizumi, T. Nishida, H. Purohit, R. Tanabe, T. Endo, M. Yamamoto et al., “Description and discussion on DCASE 2022 challenge task 2: Unsupervised anomalous sound detection for machine condition monitoring applying domain generalization techniques,” in Proc. DCASE, 2022, pp. 1–5.\n839\t\n840\t[4]\n841\t\n842\tK. Dohi, K. Imoto, N. Harada, D. Niizumi, Y. Koizumi, T. Nishida, H. Purohit, R. Tan